# OSS compliance

License inventory and policy gate via **`uv run tests compliance`** (catalpa-workspace-tooling v0.10.0+). [docs/COMPLIANCE.md](https://github.com/catalpainternational/catalpa-workspace-tooling/blob/main/docs/COMPLIANCE.md) in catalpa-workspace-tooling.

## When to suggest compliance

- User bumped **submodules** or other dependencies
- User changed platform/runtime `pyproject.toml`, Python lockfile(s), or frontend `pnpm-lock.yaml` / `yarn.lock`
- User asks about AGPL, SBOM, third-party notices, copyleft deps, or OSS policy before release

## Setup (consumer repo)

1. **`compliance:`** block in repo-root `tooling.yaml` (paths relative to consumer root; `javascript.cwd` usually matches `paths.frontend`)
2. **`[dependency-groups].compliance`** in **consumer** `pyproject.toml` — not in runtime/submodule `pyproject.toml`
3. Committed artifacts under **`compliance/`** (SBOM + `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`)

## Safe local run

```bash
uv sync
uv run tests compliance              # regenerate artifacts
uv run tests compliance --check-only # CI-style gate (no writes)
```

Use **`--ci`** in GitHub Actions for non-interactive CI runs.

## Do not

- Add `cyclonedx-bom` / `pip-licenses` to **runtime/submodule** `pyproject.toml` — forces docker lock churn with no image benefit
- Commit stale `compliance/` artifacts after dependency changes without re-running `tests compliance`
- Run network probes against **staging/prod** deploy hosts without explicit user confirmation

